## Weekend Cutover Plan

### Friday Evening: Prep & Validate

| Time | Task |
|------|------|
| 5-6 PM | Wire up your actual data stores, implement business logic, run contract tests against Go service |
| 6-7 PM | Load test with realistic traffic (use your Node.js production traffic replay or a tool like vegeta) |
| 7-8 PM | Deploy to staging, run full integration suite, verify metrics/observability parity |

**Go/No-go checkpoint**: Contract tests pass + load test shows memory/latency improvement.

### Saturday: Shadow Traffic

| Time | Task |
|------|------|
| 9 AM | Deploy Go service to production alongside Node.js (different port/internal DNS) |
| 10 AM | Configure shadow traffic: mirror live requests to Go service, discard responses |
| 12 PM | Monitor Go service memory, CPU, error logs under real traffic patterns |
| 3 PM | Compare metrics — if Go looks stable, proceed |
| 4 PM | Switch shadow to "compare mode": route 5% of traffic to Go, compare responses to Node.js |
| 6 PM | Analyze any divergence, fix if needed |

**Go/No-go checkpoint**: Shadow traffic shows matching responses with better resource profile.

### Sunday: Cutover

| Time | Task |
|------|------|
| 9 AM | Route 25% traffic to Go service via load balancer weight |
| 10 AM | Monitor p99 latency, error rates, memory |
| 11 AM | Bump to 50% if metrics look good |
| 12 PM | Bump to 75% |
| 1 PM | Bump to 100% — Node.js is now idle |
| 2 PM | Keep Node.js running but out of rotation for quick rollback |
| 5 PM | If stable for 3 hours, scale down Node.js instances |

**Rollback trigger**: Error rate > 1% or p99 latency > 2x baseline for more than 2 minutes → instant rollback to 100% Node.js.

---

## Why This Works

- **Contract tests de-risk the rewrite** — you already know the API surface is correct
- **Shadow traffic validates under real load** — no synthetic test can match production
- **Gradual traffic shift** — limits blast radius if something's wrong
- **Go's memory model** — should eliminate your Node.js memory bloat immediately
- **Single weekend** — limits context switching and keeps momentum

